<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- The full-screen cover's theme, and the first line is the whole point.
         OPAQUE, not translucent.

         The cover always fills the screen, so it has no business declaring
         otherwise - and declaring otherwise is what let the game show through
         it. Theme.Translucent tells WindowManager the window may not cover
         what is under it, so the Activity below is kept VISIBLE and
         composited underneath; any moment this window is not yet fully
         painted, the compositor fills the gap with Unity's last frame. An
         opaque window has no such gap: the system knows nothing behind it can
         show, and stops drawing the Activity below.

         The theme was inherited from the AD Activity, which genuinely needs
         translucency because a half-screen panel does not fill the screen.
         The cover took a property it never wanted.

         windowBackground black so the window is painted before the content
         view has laid out; windowAnimationStyle @null so coming back to the
         front is not an alpha fade, which would reopen the same gap for the
         length of the animation. -->
    <style name="RiseOnFullScreenCoverTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/black</item>
        <item name="android:windowAnimationStyle">@null</item>
    </style>
</resources>
